    {
      "id": "m4.3",
      "title": "Progressions you will meet everywhere",
      "summary": "I–IV–V–I and I–V–vi–IV.",
      "minutes": 10,
      "skillIds": [
        "m.cadences",
        "m.roman"
      ],
      "reviewState": "draft",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A few chord patterns turn up in thousands of pieces. The most basic is I–IV–V–I: leave home, move away, build tension, come back."
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "exercise": {
            "id": "ex39",
            "kind": "playProgression",
            "prompt": "Play I–IV–V–I in C major.",
            "chords": [
              {
                "root": "C",
                "quality": "major",
                "inversion": 0
              },
              {
                "root": "F",
                "quality": "major",
                "inversion": 0
              },
              {
                "root": "G",
                "quality": "major",
                "inversion": 0
              },
              {
                "root": "C",
                "quality": "major",
                "inversion": 0
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              "I",
              "IV",
              "V",
              "I"
            ],
            "explanation": "C – F – G – C. The move from V to I at the end is called an authentic cadence, the strongest ending in tonal music."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The ending V–I sounds finished. Ending on V instead sounds like a question and is called a half cadence. IV–I is the gentle plagal cadence, the sound of Amen."
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "exercise": {
            "id": "ex40",
            "kind": "playProgression",
            "prompt": "Play I–IV–V–I in G major.",
