    {
      "id": "m4.4",
      "title": "Seventh chords",
      "summary": "Add one more third on top of a triad.",
      "minutes": 8,
      "skillIds": [
        "m.seventhChords"
      ],
      "reviewState": "draft",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Stack one more third on a triad and you get a four-note seventh chord. The most important is the dominant seventh, built on the fifth note of the key: a major triad with a minor seventh above the root. In C major it is G7: G, B, D, F."
        },
        {
          "type": "chords",
          "chords": [
            {
              "root": "G",
              "quality": "dominant7",
              "inversion": 0
            },
            {
              "root": "C",
              "quality": "major",
              "inversion": 0
            }
          ],
          "chordLabels": [
            "V7",
            "I"
          ],
          "text": "G7 pulls to C even more strongly than plain G."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Two others are common. A major seventh chord (Cmaj7: C, E, G, B) sounds soft and open. A minor seventh chord (Dm7: D, F, A, C) is the usual chord on ii."
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "exercise": {
            "id": "ex43",
            "kind": "choice",
            "prompt": "Which notes make G7?",
            "options": [
              "G – B – D – F♯",
              "G – B♭ – D – F",
              "G – B – D – F",
              "G – B – D♯ – F"
            ],
            "answer": 2,
            "explanation": "A G major triad (G, B, D) plus F, a minor seventh above G."
          }
        },
        {
